## {{lb|en|nonstandard|British|West Country}} {{n-g|Used before all consonants.}}

=====Usage notes=====
{{mainapp|English articles#Indefinite singular articles}}
* In standard English, the article {{m|en|an}} is used before vowel sounds, while {{m|en|a}} is used before consonant sounds. Alternatively, ''an'' can be found before an unstressed syllable beginning with {{IPAfont|/h/}}, as in "an historic". The {{IPAfont|/h/}} may then become silent or is at least very weakly articulated. This usage is favoured by only 6&nbsp;% of British speakers, and is only slightly more common in writing.<ref>''Fowler's Dictionary of Modern English Usage'' (2015, {{ISBN|0199661359}}, page 2: "Before words beginning with ''h'' […] the standard modern approach is to use ''a'' (never ''an'') together with an aspirated ''h'' […], but not to demur if others use ''an'' with minimal or nil aspiration given to the following ''h'' (''an historic'' /әn (h)ɪsˈtɒrɪk/, ''an horrific'' /әn (h)ɒˈrɪfɪk/, etc.)." ''Fowler's'' goes on to source the 6&nbsp;% figure to Wells (third edition, 2008).</ref>
* Historically, {{m|en|an}} could also be found before {{m|en|one}} and before many words with initial {{angbr|u}}, {{angbr|eu}} (now pronounced with initial {{IPAchar|/juː/|/jʊ/|/jə/}}), such as {{m|en|eunuch}}, {{m|en|unique}}, and {{m|en|utility}}. This is because those initial letters were pronounced as vowels. In writing, ''an'' remained usual before such words until the 19th century—long after these words acquired initial consonant sounds in standard English. This is still occasionally seen.<ref>{{R:OED Online|a|adj|4|June 2008}}</ref>
* In the other direction, ''a'' often occurs before a vowel in nonstandard and dialectal speech and in its written representations.<ref>{{R:en:McDavid:1979|57|375}}</ref> Example: "ain't this a innerestin sitchation" (Moira Young, ''Blood Red Road'').
* The various article senses of {{m|en|a}} are all senses of {{m|en|an}}.

